============================================================================
MATCH RULES (stated verbatim, as required)
============================================================================

normalise(s): Unicode NFKC; replace the curly apostrophe U+2019 with '; casefold; strip surrounding whitespace and the characters "'`.,;:!?()[]{}<>; collapse every internal whitespace run to one space; drop a leading article 'the ', 'a ' or 'an '; drop a trailing possessive "'s".

PRIMARY (strict): a v1 entity matches a reference entity iff normalise(v1.name) == normalise(reference.name) AND v1.type == reference.type. One-to-one: each reference entity is consumed by at most one v1 entity. SECONDARY (alias-aware, reported alongside, never instead): additionally allow a match when normalise(v1.name) equals the normalisation of ANY of the reference entity's surface_forms, with the type still required to be equal.

A v1 edge matches a reference relation iff their source entities matched, their target entities matched, and the relation string is equal. Reference relations with directed=true are matched on the ORDERED pair; with directed=false, either order is accepted. Endpoints that could not be matched to a v1 entity make the relation unmatchable, and it is counted as a reference-side miss.

v1's entity set for a chapter = every canonical node with at least one row in
`mentions` for that chapter (repository.list_mentions). v1's relation set is
reported two ways: `chapter_local` = edges with first_seen_chapter == N, and
`cumulative` = every edge whose BOTH endpoints are mentioned in chapter N
regardless of when v1 first recorded it. Neither is privileged; the strict one
penalises v1 for relations it recorded in an earlier chapter, the generous one
credits v1 for relations this chapter's text may not support.

